 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.03333333333333333,
   "dur": 2.61,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "More trees, or more stars? Guess first."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 3.5,
   "dur": 8.662,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "Our galaxy has hundreds of billions of stars. Nobody counted them. Most of its weight is dark matter, so stars are a small part."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 13.1,
   "dur": 8.198,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "Now the trees. In twenty fifteen, a team put Earth's at about three trillion. That's hundreds of trees for every person alive."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 22.166666666666668,
   "dur": 12.091,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "Researchers counted trees in ground plots, then scaled up with satellite maps. Both are estimates, not exact counts, so each really means about. The honest answer is a range."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 35.5,
   "dur": 13.647,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "Now turn every star into a tree. The stars run out, and Earth's forests keep going. Trees win, by roughly eight to thirty times. Quick thing: if that made the sky feel bigger, tap like and follow."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 50.43333333333333,
   "dur": 6.495,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "There are more trees on Earth than stars in our galaxy. Big numbers hide in ordinary places."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 57.9,
   "dur": 7.805,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "Light time across our galaxy: longer than all of written history. Across a city block: about a millionth of a second."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 66.43333333333334,
   "dur": 3.661,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next: with that many stars, why is the night sky dark?"
  }
 ]
